https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/korandiz/mpa

# Strict CRC mode

By default the decoder ignores the `crc_check` field of error-protected
MPEG-1 Audio frames. Setting `Decoder.CheckCRC` to true enables strict CRC
checking.

In strict mode the decoder actually verifies the CRC of every error-protected
(`protection_bit == 0`) frame it can check. The check covers exactly the
fields defined by ISO/IEC 11172-3: the last 16 bits of the frame header,
followed by the protected audio syntax. The `crc_check` field itself,
scalefactors, quantized samples and ancillary data are not part of the
protected region and are never checksummed.

Scope and behavior:

- Layer I: the protected region is the bit allocation as actually
  transmitted. In joint stereo the subbands at and above the bound share one
  allocation, which is therefore covered only once.
- Layer II: the protected region is the bit allocation plus the
  scalefactor-selection information (SCFSI) of every allocated subband. The
  shared joint-stereo allocation is covered once; the SCFSI still appears,
  and is covered, for each channel. The allocation table selected by the
  bitrate and sampling frequency is the same one used by the decoding path.
- A frame that fails the check is discarded whole: its samples are never fed
  to the synthesis filterbank, so the per-channel synthesis history is left
  untouched. `DecodeFrame` returns a `CRCError` carrying both the stored and
  the computed CRC, identifiable with `errors.As`. The next call begins at
  the exact following frame; a pseudo-syncword inside the rejected frame's
  ancillary data cannot start a new frame.
- A mismatch is reported only after the complete frame has been read. A frame
  truncated inside its boundaries returns `io.ErrUnexpectedEOF`, and
  underlying I/O errors are passed through unchanged.
- Frames without error protection follow the ordinary decoding path in either
  mode.
- Protected Layer III frames and free-format frames have no determinable
  protected region, so strict mode returns `ErrUnsupportedCRC` for them
  rather than guessing what to verify.
- The `CheckCRC` setting is retained across the automatic reset that happens
  when `Decoder.Input` is replaced.
